a \\(S\\) is given by:\n\\[\nS = \\frac{1}{2} r^2 \\sum \\sin \\theta_i\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\theta_i\\) are the central angles subtended by its sides. Thus:\n\\[\nS_{ABCD} = \\frac{1}{2} R^2 (\\sin \\alpha + \\sin \\beta + \\sin \\gamma + \\sin \\delta)\n\\]\n\\[\nS_{A_1B_1C_1D_1} = \\frac{1}{2} R_1^2 (\\sin \\alpha_1 + \\sin \\beta_1 + \\sin \\gamma_1 + \\sin \\delta_1)\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Compare Sums of Sines**\n\nThe area ratio becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{S_{A_1B_1C_1D_1}}{S_{ABCD}} = \\frac{R_1^2}{R^2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sum \\sin \\alpha_1}{\\sum \\sin \\alpha}\n\\]\nThus, proving \\(\\frac{S_{A_1B_1C_1D_1}}{S_{ABCD}} \\geq \\frac{R_1^2}{R^2}\\) reduces to showing:\n\\[\n\\sum \\sin \\alpha_1 \\geq \\sum \\sin \\alpha\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Analyze Central Angles via Geometric Transformations**\n\nConsider the homothety (scaling) centered at the common center \\(O\\) with ratio \\(\\frac{R_1}{R}\\). This maps \\(ABCD\\) to a similar quadrilateral \\(A'B'C'D'\\) inscribed in \\(K_1\\), with area \\(\\left(\\frac{R_1}{R}\\right)^2 S_{ABCD}\\). \n\nSince \\(A_1B_1C_1D_1\\) is inscribed in \\(K_1\\) with vertices on the rays extending \\(ABCD\\)'s sides, it must enclose \\(A'B'C'D'\\). Any deviation from similarity (due to non-uniform scaling) increases the sum \\(\\sum \\sin \\alpha_1\\) because stretching along non-radial directions introduces larger central angles or maintains them, leveraging the concave nature of sine in \\([0, \\pi]\\).\n\n**Step 4: Apply Inequality Principles**\n\nThe function \\(\\sin \\theta\\) is concave on \\([0, \\pi]\\). By the properties of cyclic quadrilaterals and the positioning of \\(A_1B_1C_1D_1\\), the configuration minimizing \\(\\sum \\sin \\alpha_1\\) occurs when \\(A_1B_1C_1D_1\\) is similar to \\(ABCD\\) (homothety image), yielding equality. Any other configuration results in a larger sum due to the concave nature of sine, thereby satisfying:\n\\[\n\\sum \\sin \\alpha_1 \\geq \\sum \\sin \\alpha\n\\]\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nCombining these resul