rivative by applying the chain rule.\n\n   \\[\n   f'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx} \\left( \\ln(1+ax) \\right) - \\frac{d}{dx} (x^2)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Differentiate each term**: \n   The derivative of \\(\\ln(1+ax)\\) is \\(\\frac{a}{1+ax}\\), and the derivative of \\(x^2\\) is \\(2x\\).\n\n   \\[\n   f'(x) = \\frac{a}{1+ax} - 2x\n   \\]\n\n3. **Set the derivative to zero to find critical points**:\n   \n   \\[\n   \\frac{a}{1+ax} - 2x = 0 \\implies \\frac{a}{1+ax} = 2x \\implies a = 2x(1+ax)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\(x\\)**:\n   \n   \\[\n   a = 2x + 2ax^2 \\implies 2ax^2 + 2x - a = 0\n   \\]\n\n   This is a quadratic equation in \\(x\\):\n\n   \\[\n   2ax^2 + 2x - a = 0\n   \\]\n\n5. **Solve the quadratic equation**:\n   \n   Using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\) with \\(a = 2a\\), \\(b = 2\\), and \\(c = -a\\):\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{2^2 - 4 \\cdot 2a \\cdot (-a)}}{2 \\cdot 2a} = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 + 8a^2}}{4a} = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{4(1 + 2a^2)}}{4a} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{2a^2 + 1}}{2a}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Identify valid solution**: \n   Since \\(a > 0\\), only the positive solution \\( \\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{2a^2 + 1}}{2a} \\) lies in the interval \\((0, 1]\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{-1 - \\sqrt{2a^2 + 1}}{2a} < 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{2a^2 + 1}}{2a} > 0\n   \\]\n\n7. **Check if \\(\\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{2a^2 + 1}}{2a} < 1\\)**:\n   \n   \\[\n   \\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{2a^2 + 1}}{2a} = \\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2a^2 + 1} + 1} < 1\n   \\]\n\n8. **Determine intervals of monotonicity**:\n   Hence, the function \\(f(x)\\) is increasing on \\( \\left(0, \\frac{\\sqrt{2a^2 + 1} - 1}{2a} \\right) \\) and decreasing on \\(\\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{2a^2 + 1} - 1}{2a}, 1 \\right)\\).\n\n   Conclusion:\n   \\(\n   \\text{The function } f(x) \\text{ is increasing on } \\left(0, \\frac{\\sqrt{2a^2 + 1} - 1}{2a}\\right) \\text{ and decreasing on } \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2a^2 + 1} - 1}{2a}, 1\\right).\n   \\)\n\n#### Part (2):\n\n1. **Given the inequality**: \n   \\( \\frac{1}{n^2} + \\lambda \\geq \\ln \\left( 1 + \\frac{2}{n} \\right) \\).\n\n2. **Isolate \\(\\lambda\\)**:\n   \n   \\[\n   \\lambda \\geq \\ln \\left(1 + \\frac{2}{n}\\right) - \\frac{1}{n^2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Substituting \\( \\frac{1}{n} = x \\)**:\n   When \\( n \\in \\mathbb{N}^* \\), \\( x \\in (0, 1] \\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\lambda \\geq \\ln(1 + 2x) - x^2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Define the function**: \n   \n   \\[\n   g(x) = \\ln(1 + 2x) - x^2 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad x \\in (0, 1]\n   \\]\n\n   Note that for \\( a = 2 \\), \\(f(x) = g(x)\\). \n\n5. **Determine intervals of monotonicity for \\(g(x)\\)**:\n   \n   \\[\n   g(x) \\text{ is increasing in } \\left(0, \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\text{ and decreasing in } \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, 1\\right)\n   \\]\n\n6. **Find maximum value of \\(g(x)\\)**:\n   \n   At \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\):\n\n   \\[\n   g\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\ln 2 - \\frac{1}{4}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Determine the maximum value over valid \\(n\\)**:\n   For all positive integers \\(n\\), the maximum value is attained at \\( n = 2 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   g( \\frac{1}{2} ) = \\ln 2 - \\frac{1}{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Conclusion:\n   \\(\n   \\text{The value for \\(\\lambda\\) must be } \\lambda \\geq \\ln 2 - \\frac{1}{4}.\n   \\)\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\lambda \\geq \\ln 2 - \\frac{1}{4}}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Show that the following inclusion-exclusion formula for the maximum of the random variables \\(X_{1}, \\ldots, X_{n}\\) is valid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX_{1} \\vee \\ldots \\vee X_{n}=\\sum_{i=1}^{n} X_{i} & -\\sum_{1 \\leqslant i<j \\leqslant n} X_{i} \\wedge X_{j}+ \\\\\n& +\\sum_{1 \\leqslant i<j<k \\leqslant n} X_{i} \\wedge X_{j} \\wedge X_{k}+\\ldots+(-1)^{n+1} X_{1} \\wedge \\ldots \\wedge X_{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, derive the inclusion-exclusion formula for probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{P}\\left(A_{1} \\