angle HIJ + \\angle JEH\\) in the quadrilateral \\(HIJE\\) formed by the angle bisectors of a convex quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\), we analyze the angles at the intersection points of the bisectors.\n\n1. **Original Quadrilateral Angles**: Let the angles of the original quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) be \\(\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma, \\delta\\) with \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta = 360^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Angle Bisectors**: Each angle bisector splits the respective angle into two equal parts. For example, the bisector of \\(\\angle A\\) splits it into \\(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\) each.\n\n3. **Angles in Quadrilateral \\(HIJE\\)**:\n   - The angle at point \\(I\\) (intersection of bisectors of \\(\\beta\\) and \\(\\gamma\\)) is \\(\\angle HIJ = 180^\\circ - \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2} + \\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)\\).\n   - The angle at point \\(E\\) (intersection of bisectors of \\(\\delta\\) and \\(\\alpha\\)) is \\(\\angle JEH = 180^\\circ - \\left(\\frac{\\delta}{2} + \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)\\).\n\n4. **Sum of Opposite Angles**:\n   \\[\n   \\angle HIJ + \\angle JEH = \\left[180^\\circ - \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2} + \\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)\\right] + \\left[180^\\circ - \\left(\\frac{\\delta}{2} + \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)\\right]\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this expression:\n   \\[\n   360^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2}(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta)\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta = 360^\\circ\\):\n   \\[\n   360^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2} \\times 360^\\circ = 360^\\circ - 180^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n\nThus, the sum of the opposite angles \\(\\angle HIJ + \\angle JEH\\) in the quadrilateral \\(HIJE\\) is \\(\\boxed{180^\\circ}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven a convex quadrilateral, let the angles of the quadrilateral be $\\alpha$, $\\beta$, $\\gamma$, and $\\delta$. When the angle bisectors of these angles are drawn, they intersect to form a new quadrilateral $HIJE$. We are to find the sum of the opposite angles $\\angle HIJ + \\angle JEH$.\n