k + 2 \\implies a^4 = (5k + 2)^4 \\equiv 2^4 = 16 \\equiv 1 \\mod 5\n   \\]\n   Remainder: \\(1\\)\n\n4. **Case \\(a \\equiv 3 \\mod 5\\)**:\n   \\[\n   a = 5k + 3 \\implies a^4 = (5k + 3)^4 \\equiv 3^4 = 81 \\equiv 1 \\mod 5\n   \\]\n   Remainder: \\(1\\)\n\n5. **Case \\(a \\equiv 4 \\mod 5\\)**:\n   \\[\n   a = 5k + 4 \\implies a^4 = (5k + 4)^4 \\equiv 4^4 = 256 \\equiv 1 \\mod 5\n   \\]\n   Remainder: \\(1\\)\n\nFor negative integers, the results are the same because raising a negative number to an even power results in a positive number, which gives the same remainder as its positive counterpart.\n\nThus, the possible remainders when \\(a^4\\) is divided by 5 are \\(\\boxed{0}\\) and \\(\\boxed{1}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Case 1: \\( a \\) is a multiple of 5.**\n\n   If \\( a \\) is a multiple of 5, then \\( a \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5} \\). Therefore, we have:\n   \\[\n   a = 5k \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a^4 = (5k)^4 = 625k^4\n   \\]\n   Since \\( 625k^4 \\) is clearly a multiple of 5, the remainder when \\( a^4 \\) is divided by 5 is:\n   \\[\n   a^4 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Case 2: \\( a \\) is not a multiple of 5.**\n\n   If \\( a \\) is not a multiple of 5, then \\( a \\) can be congruent to one of the following values modulo 5: 1, 2, 3, or 4. \n\n   - For \\( a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\):\n     \\[\n     a = 5k + 1 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     a^4 \\equiv 1^4 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\n     \\]\n\n   - For \\( a \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\):\n     \\[\n     a = 5k + 2 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     a^4 \\equiv 2^4 = 16 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\n     \\]\n\n   - For \\( a \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\):\n     \\[\n     a = 5k + 3 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     a^4 \\equiv 3^4 = 81 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\n     \\]\n\n   - For \\( a \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\):\n     \\[\n     a = 5k + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     a^4 \\e