}\n   \\]\n\n   Since $BD$ is the diameter, the radius $r$ is half the diameter:\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{BD}{2} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{2} = \\sqrt{5}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{5}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( \\alpha \\) and \\( \\beta \\) be any two interior angles of a triangle. If there exist \\( \\alpha \\) and \\( \\beta \\) such that the inequality\n\\[ \\cos \\sqrt{\\alpha} + \\cos \\sqrt{\\beta} > a + \\cos \\sqrt{\\alpha \\beta} \\]\nholds, find the range of the real number \\( a \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this problem here: given a triangle with two interior angles \u03b1 and \u03b2, we need to find the range of the real number a such that the inequality cos\u221a\u03b1 + cos\u221a\u03b2 > a + cos\u221a(\u03b1\u03b2) holds. Hmm, interesting. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, since \u03b1 and \u03b2 are interior angles of a triangle, their sum must be less than 180 degrees because the third angle will account for the remaining. So, \u03b1 + \u03b2 < \u03c0 radians (since 180 degrees is \u03c0 radians). That's a key point. Also, each angle must be greater than 0 and less than \u03c0, right? So, 0 < \u03b1, \u03b2 < \u03c0, and \u03b1 + \u03b2 < \u03c0. Wait, actually, the third angle is \u03c0 - \u03b1 - \u03b2, so all three angles must be positive. So, \u03b1 + \u03b2 < \u03c0, and individually \u03b1, \u03b2 are positive. Got it.\n\nThe inequality given is cos\u221a\u03b1 + cos\u221a\u03b2 > a + cos\u221a(\u03b1\u03b2). We need to find the range of a for which there exist \u03b1 and \u03b2 in the allowed domain (i.e., 0 < \u03b1, \u03b2 < \u03c0 and \u03b1 + \u03b2 < \u03c0) such that this inequality holds. So, essentially, we need to find the supremum (least upper bound) of cos\u221a\u03b1 + cos\u221a\u03b2 - cos\u221a(\u03b1\u03b2) over all valid \u03b1 and \u03b2, and then the range of a would be all real numbers