} + 2 \\mathcal{S}_{2,1}$ will strictly decrease each time.\n\n4. **Conclusion for Pentagone:**\n   - Being a decreasing function in each step and considering it is bound to integer values, the number of such operations is finite. Hence, every sequence of operations must end.\n   \n   \\(\\boxed{\\text{Finite sequence in pentagone case}}\\).\n\n#### Case of the 2019-gone (or generally $(2n + 1)$-gone):\n1. **General Setup:**\n   - Consider a $(2n + 1)$-gone (e.g., 2019 vertices labeled cyclically).\n   - Repeat the process initial setup similar to the pentagone.\n\n2. **Maintaining Invariants:**\n   - Consider sums $\\mathcal{S}_1 = \\sum_{k=0}^{2n} x_k$ and $\\mathcal{S}_{2, \\ell} = \\sum_{k=0}^{2n} x_k x_{k+\\ell}$.\n   - Verify that $\\mathcal{S}_1$ retains its value due to the same reasoning as in pentagone.\n   \n3. **Expression of $\\Delta$:**\n   - Construct a similar functional sum for multiple elements:\n     \\[\n     \\Delta = \\sum_{\\ell=1}^{n-1} (n-\\ell) \\mathcal{S}_{2, \\ell}^{\\prime} + \\frac{n(7 - n^2)}{6} \\mathcal{S}_{2, 0}^{\\prime}.\n     \\]\n   - Analyzing the change reveals that this amount can decrease strictly.\n\n4. **Refinement through Absolute Sums:**\n   - Define sums $\\mathcal{T}_{k,\\ell} = x_k + x_{k+1} + \\cdots + x_\\ell$.\n   - Recognize conditions where $\\mathcal{T}_{k,\\ell}$ varies minimally:\n     - If $-1,0,1$ are in the chosen ones, analyze the changes.\n     - Rewriting $\\Delta$ as $\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} \\sum_{\\ell=0}^{n-1} \\left| \\mathcal{T}_{k,\\ell} \\right|$, study $\\mathcal{T}_{0,0}$ and $\\mathcal{T}_{1, -1}$:\n     \\[\n     \\left| \\mathcal{S}_1 + x_0 \\right| - \\left| \\mathcal{S}_1 - x_0 \\right|,\n     \\]\n     which strictly decreases given $x_0 < 0$ and $\\mathcal{S}_1 > 0$.\n\n5. **Final Conclusion:**\n   - A similar invariant decrease $\\Delta$ confirms that operations yield a finite sequence.\n\n   \\(\\boxed{\\text{Finite sequence in $(2n+1)$-gone case}}\\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_