  \nSimilarly, f(3) - f(2) = (9a + 3b + c) - (4a + 2b + c) = 5a + b  \n\nBut since f(1), f(2), f(3) can be \u00b11, their differences can be 0, 2, or -2. Wait, but if f(1) and f(2) are both 1 or both -1, then their difference is 0. If one is 1 and the other is -1, the difference is 2 or -2. Similarly for f(3) - f(2). \n\nHmm, maybe this approach complicates things. Let me think again.\n\nAlternatively, since we have three equations:\n\n1) a + b + c = s1  \n2) 4a + 2b + c = s2  \n3) 9a + 3b + c = s3  \n\nwhere each s1, s2, s3 is either 1 or -1. Then, we can solve this system for each combination of s1, s2, s3 and check if a > 0.\n\nBut solving 8 systems might be tedious, but maybe manageable.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a pattern in the equations. Let's subtract the first equation from the second and the second from the third.\n\nFrom equation 2 - equation 1:  \n(4a + 2b + c) - (a + b + c) = s2 - s1  \n3a + b = s2 - s1  \n\nFrom equation 3 - equation 2:  \n(9a + 3b + c) - (4a + 2b + c) = s3 - s2  \n5a + b = s3 - s2  \n\nNow we have two equations:\n\n3a + b = s2 - s1 ...(4)  \n5a + b = s3 - s2 ...(5)  \n\nSubtracting equation (4) from equation (5):  \n(5a + b) - (3a + b) = (s3 - s2) - (s2 - s1)  \n2a = s3 - 2s2 + s1  \nThus, a = (s3 - 2s2 + s1)/2  \n\nSince a > 0, the numerator must be positive. So, s3 - 2s2 + s1 > 0.\n\nGiven that each s1, s2, s3 is \u00b11, let's compute possible values of the numerator:\n\nPossible combinations:\n\nCase 1: s1=1, s2=1, s3=1  \nNumerator: 1 - 2(1) + 1 = 1 - 2 + 1 = 0 \u2192 a = 0, which is invalid.\n\nCase 2: s1=1, s2=1, s3=-1  \nNumerator: -1 -2(1) +1 = -1 -2 +1 = -2 \u2192 a = -1, invalid.\n\nCase3: s1=1, s2=-1, s3=1  \nNumerator:1 -2(-1) +1 =1 +2 +1=4 \u2192 a=2, valid.\n\nCase4: s1=1, s2=-1, s3=-1  \nNumerator:-1 -2(-1)+1=-1+2+1=2 \u2192a=1, valid.\n\nCase5: s1=-1, s2=1, s3=1  \nNumerator:1 -2(1) + (-1)=1 -2 -1=-2\u2192a=-1, invalid.\n\nCase6: s1=-1, s2=1, s3=-1  \nNumerator:-1 -2(1)+(-1)=-1-2-1=-4\u2192a=-2, invalid.\n\nCase7: s1=-1, s2=-1, s3=1  \nNumerator:1 -2(-1) + (-1)=1+2-1=2\u2192a=1, valid.\n\nCase8: s1=-1, s2=-1, s3=-1  \nNumerator:-1 -2(-1) + (-1)=-1+2-1=0\u2192a=0, invalid.\n\nSo the valid cases where a >0 are Cases3,4,7.\n\nSo, for these three cases, let's compute a, b, c.\n\nStarting with Case3: s1=1, s2=-1, s3=1  \nCompute a =4/2=2  \nFrom equation (4):3a + b = s2 - s1 \u2192 3*2 + b = -1 -1 \u21926 + b = -2 \u2192b = -8  \nThen from equation1: a + b + c = s1 \u21922 + (-8) + c =1 \u2192-6 +c=1\u2192c=7  \nSo coefficients: a=2, b=-8, c=7. Let's check if f(1)=2 -8 +7=1, yes. f(2)=8 -16 +7=-1, yes. f(3)=18 -24 +7=1, yes. So that works. Also a=2>0.\n\nCase4: s1=1, s2=-1, s3=-1  \na=2/2=1  \nFrom equation (4):3*1 + b = s2 -s1 \u21923 + b = -1 -1\u21923 +b =-2\u2192b=-5  \nFrom equation1:1 + (-5) +c=1\u2192-4 +c=1\u2192c=5  \nCheck f(1)=1 -5 +5=1, yes. f(2)=4 -10 +5=-1, yes. f(3)=9 -15 +5=-1, yes. So coefficients a=1, b=-5, c=5. Valid.\n\nCase7: s1=-1, s2=-1, s3=1  \na=2/2=1  \nFrom equation(4):3*1 +b =s2 -s1 \u21923 +b = -1 -(-1)= -1 +1=0\u2192b= -3  \nFrom equation1: a +b +c =s1 \u21921 + (-3) +c= -1\u2192 -2 +c= -1\u2192c=1  \nCheck f(1)=1 -3 +1=-1, yes. f(2)=4 -6 +1=-1, yes. f(3)=9 -9 +1=1, yes. So coefficients a=1, b=-3, c=1. Valid.\n\nTherefore, there are three possible quadratics:\n\n1. a=2, b=-8, c=7  \n2. a=1, b=-5, c=5  \n3. a=1, b=-3, c=1  \n\nWait, but let me check if there are any other cases. Because maybe when I considered the numerator, perhaps I missed something. Let me double-check the cases:\n\nWe had 8 possible combinations of s1, s2, s3. Then, computed the numerator s3 - 2s2 + s1 and only cases where that numerator is positive were considered. Let me verify the calculations for each case again:\n\nCase1: (1,1,1) \u2192 1 -2(1) +1=0\u2192invalid  \nCase2: (1,1,-1)\u2192-1-2(1)+1=-2\u2192a=-1 invalid  \nCase3: (1,-1,1)\u21921-2(-1)+1=4\u2192a=2 valid  \nCase4: (1,-1,-1)\u2192-1-2(-1)+1=2\u2192a=1 valid  \nCase5: (-1,1,1)\u21921-2(1)+(-1)=-2\u2192a=-1 invalid  \nCase6: (-1,1,-1)\u2192-1-2(1)+(-1)=-4\u2192a=-2 invalid  \nCase7: (-1,-1,1)\u21921-2(-1)+(-1)=2\u2192a=1 valid  \nCase8: (-1,-1,-1)\u2192-1-2(-1)+(-1)=0\u2192a=0 invalid  \n\nYes, seems correct. So only three cases give a>0.\n\nNow, let me check if these quadratics indeed satisfy |f(1)|=|f(2)|=|f(3)|=1.\n\nFirst quadratic: 2x\u00b2 -8x +7  \nf(1)=2 -8 +7=1  \nf(2)=8 -16 +7=-1  \nf(3)=18 -24 +7=1  \nAbsolute values all 1. Good.\n\nSecond quadratic: x\u00b2 -5x +5  \nf(1)=1 -5 +5=1  \nf(2)=4 -10 +5=-1  \nf(3)=9 -15 +5=-1  \nAbsolute values:1,1,1. Wait, but f(3)=-1, so absolute value 1. Correct.\n\nThird quadratic: x\u00b2 -3x +1  \nf(1)=1 -3 +1=-1  \nf(2)=4 -6 +1=-1  \nf(3)=9 -9 +1=1  \nAbsolute values all 1. Correct.\n\nSo these three quadratics satisfy the conditions. Are there any others? According to the analysis, only these three. Let me confirm.\n\nWait, but in the case where s1=1, s2=-1, s3=-1 (case4), the quadratic is x\u00b2 -5x +5. Let's compute f(3)=9 -15 +5=-1, which is absolute 1. So yes. Similarly for the third case.\n\nTherefore, these are the three possible quadratics. However, wait a second. Let me check if there are other possible combinations where s3 -2s2 +s1 could be positive. For example, if s1=-1, s2=-1, s3=1, then s3 -2s2 +s1=1 -2*(-1)+(-1)=1+2-1=2, which is positive, leading to a=1. That's case7, which we considered.\n\nBut, is there any case where s3 -2s2 +s1 is positive but s1, s2, s3 are not in the three cases above? Let's see. For example, if s1=1, s2=-1, s3=1: which gives a=2, and that's case3. Similarly, if s1=1, s2=-1, s3=-1: a=1. And s1=-1, s2=-1, s3=1: a=1. All others give a <=0. So yes, three cases.\n\nBut wait, what if the equations are set up with different combinations? For example, maybe f(1)=1, f(2)=1, f(3)=-1. But in this case, the numerator would be s3 -2s2 +s1= -1 -2(1)+1= -1-2+1=-2, leading to a=-1, which is invalid. So even if we try other combinations, they don't yield a positive a. So only three possible quadratics.\n\nTherefore, the possible coefficients are:\n\n1. a=2, b=-8, c=7  \n2. a=1, b=-5, c=5  \n3. a=1, b=-3, c=1  \n\nBut let me check if these quadratics open upwards (since a>0), which they do, as a=2 and a=1 in all cases.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"What could be the values of its coefficients?\" So there are three possible sets of coefficients. Therefore, these are the solutions.\n\nBut let me see if I missed any cases. For example, consider s1=1, s2=1, s3=-1. The numerator is -1 -2(1) +1 = -2, so a=-1, which is invalid. Similarly, other combinations give a <=0. So yes, only the three cases.\n\nThus, the answer should list these three possibilities.", "deepseek_solution": "The quadratic polynomial \\( f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \\) with \\( a > 0 \\) satisfies \\( |f(1)| = |f(2)| = |f(3)| = 1 \\). By analyzing all possible sign combinations for \\( f(1), f(2), f(3) \\) and solving the resulting systems, the valid coefficient sets are:\n\n1. **\\( a = 2, \\, b = -8, \\, c = 7 \\)**  \n   - \\( f(1) = 1, \\, f(2) = -1, \\, f(3) = 1 \\)\n\n2. **\\( a = 1, \\, b = -5, \\, c = 5 \\)**  \n   - \\( f(1) = 1, \\, f(2) = -1, \\, f(3) = -1 \\)\n\n3. **\\( a = 1, \\, b = -3, \\, c = 1 \\)**  \n   - \\( f(1) = -1, \\, f(2) = -1, \\, f(3) = 1 \\)\n\nThese are the only solutions where \\( a > 0 \\), and all absolute value conditions are satisfied.\n\n**Final Answer**  \nThe possible coefficients are:  \n\\(\\boxed{a = 2, \\, b = -8, \\, c = 7}\\),  \n\\(\\boxed{a = 1, \\, b = -5, \\, c = 5}\\), and  \n\\(\\boxed{a = 1, \\, b = -3, \\, c = 1}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven a quadratic polynomial \\( f(x)=a x^{2}+b x+c \\), where \\( a > 0 \\), it is known that:\n\n\\[ |f(1)|=|f(2)|=|f(3)|=1 \\]\n\nWe are asked to find the possible values of the coefficients \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\).\n\n#### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Identify Properties and Symmetry**:\n   A quadratic function can take a specific value at most twice, thus the values \\( f(1) \\), \\( f(2) \\), and \\( f(3) \\) cannot all be of the same sign. As they must be equal to \\(\\pm 1\\), exactly two out of \\(f(1)\\), \\(f(2)\\), and \\(f(3)\\) will have the same value.\n\n2. **Formulate Equations**:\n   We have the following conditions:\n   \\[\n   \\left\\{\n   \\begin{align*}\n   |f(1)| & = 1 \\\\\n   |f(2)| & = 1 \\\\\n   |f(3)| & = 1\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\right.\n   \\]\n   Since the quadratic takes the form \\( f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \\), we write:\n   \\[\n   \\left\\{\n   \\begin{align*}\n   |a + b + c| & = 1 \\\\\n   |4a + 2b + c| & = 1 \\\\\n   |9a + 3b + c| & = 1\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\right.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Possible Cases**:\n   Considering that the quadratic polynomial could take values \\(\\pm 1\\) in these specific points, the following cases emerge:\n   - \\(f(1) = 1\\), \\(f(2) = -1\\), \\(f(3) = 1\\)\n   - \\(f(1) = -1\\), \\(f(2) = 1\\), \\(f(3) = -1\\)\n   - \\(f(1) = 1\\), \\(f(2) = 1\\), \\(f(3) = -1\\)\n   - \\(f(1) = -1\\), \\(f(2) = -1\\), \\(f(3) = 1\\)\n  \nGiven that the parabola opens upwards (\\(a > 0\\)), viable symmetrical cases occur around the vertex:\n\n4. **Solving Each Possible Case**:\n   We consider symmetry around \\(x = \\dfrac{-b}{2a}\\) (vertex form), leading us to:\n   - \\( x = 2 \\), \n   - \\( x = 1.5 \\), \n   - \\( x = 2.5 \\).\n\nFor instance, we consider:\n\\[ f(1) = 1, f(2) = -1, f(3) = 1 \\]\n   This translates into the system:\n   \\[\n   \\left\\{\n   \\begin{align*}\n   a + b + c & = 1, \\\\\n   4a + 2b + c & = -1, \\\\\n   9a + 3b + c & = 1.\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\right.\n   \\]\n\n- **Finding the Coefficients**:\n\n   Solving the system of equations, for instance, the first case:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   a + b + c = 1 \\\\\n   4a + 2b + c = -1 \\\\\n   9a + 3b + c = 1\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Subtract the first equation from the second:\n   \\[\n   (4a + 2b + c) - (a + b + c) = -1 - 1 \\implies 3a + b = -2 \\quad (1)\n   \\]\n\n   Subtract the second equation from the third:\n   \\[\n   (9a + 3b + c) - (4a + 2b + c) = 1 + 1 \\implies 5a + b = 2 \\quad (2)\n   \\]\n\n   Solving the two simplified linear equations:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   3a + b = -2 \\\\\n   5a + b = 2\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   Subtract the first from the second:\n   \\[\n   (5a + b) - (3a + b) = 2 + 2 \\implies 2a = 4 \\implies a = 2\n   \\]\n\n   Substitute \\( a = 2 \\) into \\( 3a + b = -2 \\):\n   \\[\n   3(2) + b = -2 \\implies 6 + b = -2 \\implies b = -8\n   \\]\n\n   Finally, substituting \\( a = 2 \\) and \\( b = -8 \\) into \\( a + b + c = 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   2 - 8 + c = 1 \\implies -6 + c = 1 \\implies c = 7\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThus, the possible coefficient sets \\( (a, b, c) \\) are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\left\\{\n\\begin{aligned}\n(a, b, c) & = (2, -8, 7), \\\\\n(a, b, c) & = (1, -3, 1), \\\\\n(a, b, c) & = (1, -5, 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\right\\}\n}\n\\]\n", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "If a line \\( l \\) is intersected by two other lines \\( l_{1}: 4x + y + 6 = 0 \\) and \\( l_{2}: 3x - 5y - 6 = 0 \\) such that the midpoint of the segment cut off by the two lines is exactly at the origin, what is the equation of line \\( l \\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the equation of line \\( l \\) that is intersected by lines \\( l_1: 4x + y + 6 = 0 \\) and \\( l_2: 3x - 5y - 6 = 0 \\). The key condition here is that the midpoint of the segment cut off by these two lines on \\( l \\) is exactly at the origin. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this step by step.\n\nFirst, let's visualize the problem. We have two lines \\( l_1 \\) and \\( l_2 \\), and another line \\( l \\) that intersects both of them. The points where \\( l \\) intersects \\( l_1 \\) and \\( l_2 \\) form a segment, and the midpoint of this segment is the origin (0,0). So, if I can find the two points of intersection between \\( l \\) and \\( l_1 \\), and \\( l \\) and \\( l_2 \\), their midpoint should be the origin. \n\nLet me denote the points of intersection as \\( P \\) (intersection with \\( l_1 \\)) and \\( Q \\) (intersection with \\( l_2 \\)). Then, the midpoint \\( M \\) of segment \\( PQ \\) is given by \\( M = \\left( \\frac{x_P + x_Q}{2}, \\frac{y_P + y_Q}{2} \\right) \\). According to the problem, this midpoint is (0,0), so:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x_P + x_Q}{2} = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{y_P + y_Q}{2} = 0\n\\]\n\nWhich implies:\n\n\\[\nx_P + x_Q = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y_P + y_Q = 0\n\\]\n\nSo, the coordinates of \\( Q \\) are the negatives of the coordinates of \\( P \\), or vice versa. That is, \\( Q = (-x_P, -y_P) \\). \n\nBut \\( P \\) lies on \\( l_1 \\), so it must satisfy \\( 4x_P + y_P + 6 = 0 \\). Similarly, \\( Q \\) lies on \\( l_2 \\), so substituting \\( Q = (-x_P, -y_P) \\) into \\( l_2 \\)'s equation:\n\n\\[\n3(-x_P) - 5(-y_P) - 6 = 0 \\\\\n-3x_P + 5y_P - 6 = 0\n\\]\n\nSo now I have two equations:\n\n1. \\( 4x_P + y_P + 6 = 0 \\)\n2. \\( -3x_P + 5y_P - 6 = 0 \\)\n\nThese are two linear equations with two variables \\( x_P \\) and \\( y_P \\). Let me solve this system to find \\( x_P \\) and \\( y_P \\).\n\nLet me write them again:\n\n1. \\( 4x + y = -6 \\) (Equation 1)\n2. \\( -3x + 5y = 6 \\) (Equation 2)\n\nI can use the elimination method. Let's multiply Equation 1 by 5 to eliminate \\( y \\):\n\n\\( 20x + 5y = -30 \\) (Equation 1a)\n\nNow subtract Equation 2 from Equation 1a:\n\n\\( (20x + 5y) - (-3x + 5y) = -30 - 6 \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( 20x + 5y + 3x - 5y = -36 \\)\n\nWhich becomes:\n\n\\( 23x = -36 \\)\n\nSo, \\( x = -36/23 \\). \n\nNow plug this back into Equation 1 to find \\( y \\):\n\n\\( 4(-36/23) + y = -6 \\)\n\nCalculate:\n\n\\( -144/23 + y = -6 \\)\n\nConvert -6 to 23rds:\n\n\\( -6 = -138/23 \\)\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\( y = -138/23 + 144/23 = 6/23 \\)\n\nSo, the coordinates of point \\( P \\) are \\( (-36/23, 6/23) \\). Then, point \\( Q \\) must be \\( (36/23, -6/23) \\).\n\nNow, since both points \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\) lie on line \\( l \\), we can find the equation of line \\( l \\) by determining the line that passes through these two points.\n\nLet me calculate the slope \\( m \\) of line \\( l \\):\n\n\\( m = \\frac{y_Q - y_P}{x_Q - x_P} = \\frac{(-6/23 - 6/23)}{(36/23 - (-36/23))} = \\frac{(-12/23)}{(72/23)} = \\frac{-12}{72} = -1/6 \\)\n\nSo, the slope of line \\( l \\) is \\( -1/6 \\).\n\nNow, using point-slope form with point \\( P \\):\n\n\\( y - y_P = m(x - x_P) \\)\n\nPlugging in:\n\n\\( y - 6/23 = -1/6 (x + 36/23) \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by 23 to eliminate denominators:\n\n\\( 23(y - 6/23) = -1/6 (23x + 36) \\)\n\nSimplify left side:\n\n\\( 23y - 6 = -1/6 (23x + 36) \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by 6 to eliminate the fraction:\n\n\\( 6(23y - 6) = - (23x + 36) \\)\n\nCalculate left side:\n\n\\( 138y - 36 = -23x - 36 \\)\n\nAdd 36 to both sides:\n\n\\( 138y = -23x \\)\n\nDivide both sides by 23:\n\n\\( 6y = -x \\)\n\nRearrange to standard form:\n\n\\( x + 6y = 0 \\)\n\nWait, let me check that again because when I did point-slope, maybe I made a miscalculation. Let me go back step by step.\n\nOriginal points:\n\n\\( P(-36/23, 6/23) \\) and \\( Q(36/23, -6/23) \\)\n\nSlope between them:\n\n\\( m = (-6/23 - 6/23)/(36/23 + 36/23) = (-12/23)/(72/23) = -12/72 = -1/6 \\). That's correct.\n\nUsing point-slope with point P:\n\n\\( y - 6/23 = -1/6 (x - (-36/23)) \\)\n\nSo,\n\n\\( y - 6/23 = -1/6 (x + 36/23) \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by 138 (which is 6*23) to eliminate denominators:\n\n138*(y - 6/23) = -138*(1/6)(x + 36/23)\n\nSimplify left side:\n\n138y - (138*6)/23 = ?\n\n138/23 is 6, so 6*6=36. So left side is 138y - 36.\n\nRight side:\n\n-138*(1/6)(x + 36/23) = -23*(x + 36/23)\n\nBecause 138/6=23.\n\nSo right side is -23x - (23*36)/23 = -23x -36.\n\nSo equation is:\n\n138y -36 = -23x -36\n\nAdd 36 to both sides:\n\n138y = -23x\n\nDivide both sides by 23:\n\n6y = -x\n\nWhich gives x + 6y = 0, or equivalently, x = -6y. So the equation of line \\( l \\) is \\( x + 6y = 0 \\).\n\nBut let me check if this line actually passes through both points \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\).\n\nFor point \\( P(-36/23, 6/23) \\):\n\nPlug into x + 6y:\n\n-36/23 + 6*(6/23) = -36/23 + 36/23 = 0. Correct.\n\nFor point \\( Q(36/23, -6/23) \\):\n\n36/23 + 6*(-6/23) = 36/23 -36/23 = 0. Correct.\n\nSo the equation of line \\( l \\) is \\( x + 6y = 0 \\).\n\nBut let me verify another way. Since we know two points on the line, we can also write the equation using the two-point form.\n\nThe two points are \\( (-36/23, 6/23) \\) and \\( (36/23, -6/23) \\).\n\nThe two-point form is:\n\n\\( \\frac{y - y_1}{y_2 - y_1} = \\frac{x - x_1}{x_2 - x_1} \\)\n\nPlugging in \\( (x_1, y_1) = (-36/23, 6/23) \\) and \\( (x_2, y_2) = (36/23, -6/23) \\):\n\n\\( \\frac{y - 6/23}{-6/23 - 6/23} = \\frac{x - (-36/23)}{36/23 - (-36/23)} \\)\n\nSimplify denominators:\n\nLeft denominator: -12/23\n\nRight denominator: 72/23\n\nSo:\n\n\\( \\frac{y - 6/23}{-12/23} = \\frac{x + 36/23}{72/23} \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by 23 to eliminate denominators:\n\n\\( \\frac{y - 6/23}{-12} = \\frac{x + 36/23}{72} \\)\n\nCross-multiplying:\n\n72(y - 6/23) = -12(x + 36/23)\n\nDivide both sides by 12:\n\n6(y - 6/23) = - (x + 36/23)\n\nExpand:\n\n6y - 36/23 = -x - 36/23\n\nAdd 36/23 to both sides:\n\n6y = -x\n\nSo, x + 6y = 0. Same result. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, since the line passes through the origin? Wait, the midpoint is the origin, but the line itself may or may not pass through the origin. Wait, but in this case, the line equation we found is \\( x + 6y = 0 \\), which does pass through the origin. Let me check.\n\nIf x=0, y=0 satisfies the equation. Yes. So line \\( l \\) passes through the origin. But the midpoint of segment PQ is the origin, but PQ is the segment between the two intersection points. So line \\( l \\) passes through the origin and the two points P and Q which are symmetric with respect to the origin. So that makes sense. So line \\( l \\) is passing through the origin and is such that when it intersects \\( l_1 \\) and \\( l_2 \\), the points are negatives of each other. Hence, the line passes through the origin. Therefore, the equation is \\( x + 6y = 0 \\).\n\nBut let me think again if there could be another line that doesn't pass through the origin but still has the midpoint at the origin. Wait, if the midpoint is the origin, then the average of the coordinates is (0,0), which implies that the coordinates of Q are (-x_P, -y_P). Therefore, if line \\( l \\) passes through both P and Q, which are (-x_P, -y_P) and (x_P, y_P), then the line must pass through the origin. Because if a line passes through two points that are symmetric with respect to the origin, then the origin lies on that line. Let me confirm that.\n\nSuppose a line passes through (a, b) and (-a, -b). Then the slope is (-b - b)/(-a - a) = (-2b)/(-2a) = b/a. The equation of the line is y - b = (b/a)(x - a). Simplifying: y - b = (b/a)x - b, so y = (b/a)x. Which passes through (0,0). Therefore, yes, such a line must pass through the origin. Therefore, line \\( l \\) must pass through the origin, so its equation is of the form y = mx, or equivalently, ax + by = 0. \n\nTherefore, in this problem, once we found that the line passes through the origin, we could have assumed the general equation as y = mx and found m such that the intersections with \\( l_1 \\) and \\( l_2 \\) satisfy the midpoint condition. Let me try that approach as a cross-check.\n\nLet line \\( l \\) be \\( y = mx \\). Find its intersection with \\( l_1: 4x + y + 6 = 0 \\).\n\nSubstitute \\( y = mx \\) into \\( l_1 \\):\n\n4x + mx + 6 = 0\n\nx(4 + m) = -6\n\nx = -6 / (4 + m)\n\nThen y = m * (-6 / (4 + m)) = -6m / (4 + m)\n\nSo point P is (-6/(4 + m), -6m/(4 + m))\n\nSimilarly, find intersection with \\( l_2: 3x - 5y - 6 = 0 \\)\n\nSubstitute \\( y = mx \\):\n\n3x -5mx -6 = 0\n\nx(3 -5m) =6\n\nx=6/(3 -5m)\n\nThen y = m *6/(3 -5m) = 6m/(3 -5m)\n\nSo point Q is (6/(3 -5m), 6m/(3 -5m))\n\nSince the midpoint of PQ is the origin, the average of x-coordinates is 0:\n\n[ (-6/(4 + m) ) + (6/(3 -5m)) ] / 2 = 0\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\n(-6/(4 + m)) + (6/(3 -5m)) = 0\n\nMultiply through by (4 + m)(3 -5m) to eliminate denominators:\n\n-6(3 -5m) +6(4 + m) = 0\n\nExpand:\n\n-18 +30m +24 +6m =0\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n( -18 +24 ) + (30m +6m) =0\n\n6 +36m=0\n\n36m= -6\n\nm= -6/36= -1/6\n\nTherefore, slope m= -1/6, so equation of line \\( l \\) is y= -1/6 x, which can be rewritten as x +6y=0, same as before. So this confirms the previous result.\n\nTherefore, the equation of line \\( l \\) is \\( x +6y=0 \\).\n\nAnother way to approach the problem is by parametric equations or vector methods, but since we've already confirmed it through two different methods (solving coordinates directly and assuming the line passes through the origin), and both give the same result, I can be confident that this is the correct answer.\n\nBut just to ensure thoroughness, let's consider if there's any possibility that line \\( l \\) is not passing through the origin but still the midpoint is the origin. Wait, earlier we concluded that if the midpoint is the origin, then the points are negatives, hence line passes through origin. So that is a general result. Hence, any line that has a segment between two points with midpoint at the origin must pass through the origin if those two points are negatives of each other. Wait, but actually, no. Suppose the line doesn't pass through the origin, but the midpoint of two points on the line is the origin. For example, take two points (a,b) and (-a,-b). The midpoint is (0,0), and the line passing through these points must pass through the origin because (a,b) and (-a,-b) are colinear with the origin. Therefore, the line must pass through the origin. Therefore, in such a case, the line must pass through the origin. Therefore, in this problem, line \\( l \\) must pass through the origin. Therefore, the equation is of the form \\( y = mx \\), and we can find m accordingly, as done above. So this confirms that the only possible line is \\( x +6y=0 \\).\n\nTherefore, after thorough analysis and multiple methods of verification, the equation of line \\( l \\) is indeed \\( x +6y=0 \\).", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the equation of line \\( l \\) such that the midpoint of the segment cut off by lines \\( l_1: 4x + y + 6 = 0 \\) and \\( l_2: 3x - 5y - 6 = 0 \\) is the origin, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Define Intersection Points**:\n   - Let \\( P = (x_P, y_P) \\) be the intersection of \\( l \\) with \\( l_1 \\).\n   - Let \\( Q = (x_Q, y_Q) \\) be the intersection of \\( l \\) with \\( l_2 \\).\n   - The midpoint of \\( PQ \\) is the origin: \\( \\left( \\frac{x_P + x_Q}{2}, \\frac{y_P + y_Q}{2} \\right) = (0,0) \\), implying \\( x_Q = -x_P \\) and \\( y_Q = -y_P \\).\n\n2. **Solve for Coordinates of \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\)**:\n   - Substitute \\( P \\) into \\( l_1 \\): \\( 4x_P + y_P + 6 = 0 \\).\n   - Substitute \\( Q = (-x_P, -y_P) \\) into \\( l_2 \\): \\( -3x_P + 5y_P - 6 = 0 \\).\n   - Solve the system:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{cases}\n     4x_P + y_P = -6 \\\\\n     -3x_P + 5y_P = 6\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\]\n     Multiply the first equation by 5 and subtract the second:\n     \\[\n     23x_P = -36 \\implies x_P = -\\frac{36}{23}\n     \\]\n     Substitute \\( x_P \\) back into \\( 4x_P + y_P = -6 \\):\n     \\[\n     y_P = \\frac{6}{23}\n     \\]\n   - Thus, \\( P = \\left( -\\frac{36}{23}, \\frac{6}{23} \\right) \\) and \\( Q = \\left( \\frac{36}{23}, -\\frac{6}{23} \\right) \\).\n\n3. **Determine the Equation of Line \\( l \\)**:\n   - Calculate the slope \\( m \\) between \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\):\n     \\[\n     m = \\frac{-\\frac{6}{23} - \\frac{6}{23}}{\\frac{36}{23} - \\left(-\\frac{36}{23}\\right)} = \\frac{-\\frac{12}{23}}{\\frac{72}{23}} = -\\frac{1}{6}\n     \\]\n   - Use point-slope form with \\( P \\):\n     \\[\n     y - \\frac{6}{23} = -\\frac{1}{6}\\left(x + \\frac{36}{23}\\right)\n     \\]\n     Simplify to standard form:\n     \\[\n     x + 6y = 0\n     \\]\n\n**Final Answer**: The equation of line \\( l \\) is \\(\\boxed{x + 6y = 0}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "Consider two lines \\(l_1\\) and \\(l_2\\) given by the equations:\n\\[ l_1: 4x + y + 6 = 0 \\]\n\\[ l_2: 3x - 5y - 6 = 0 \\]\n\n1. Let's find the intersection point \\((x_0, y_0)\\) of the lines \\(l_1\\) and \\(l_2\\). We solve the system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n4x_0 + y_0 + 6 = 0 \\quad \\text{(1)} \\\\\n3x_0 - 5y_0 - 6 = 0 \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n2. Substitute \\(y_0\\) from equation (1):\n\\[\ny_0 = -4x_0 - 6\n\\]\n\n3. Substitute \\(y_0\\) in equation (2):\n\\[\n3x_0 - 5(-4x_0 - 6) - 6 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n3x_0 + 20x_0 + 30 - 6 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n23x_0 + 24 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n23x_0 = -24 \\implies x_0 = -\\frac{24}{23}\n\\]\n\n4. Substitute \\(x_0\\) into the expression for \\(y_0\\):\n\\[\ny_0 = -4\\left(-\\frac{24}{23}\\right) - 6\n\\]\n\\[\ny_0 = \\frac{96}{23} - 6\n\\]\n\\[\ny_0 = \\frac{96}{23} - \\frac{138}{23}\n\\]\n\\[\ny_0 = \\frac{96 - 138}{23} = \\frac{-42}{23}\n\\]\n\n5. The intersection point \\((x_0, y_0)\\) is:\n\\[\n(x_0, y_0) = \\left(-\\frac{24}{23}, \\frac{-42}{23}\\right)\n\\]\n\n6. Since this point is the midpoint, it has to lie on the line \\(l\\) and the midpoint is given as the origin \\((0, 0)\\). The midpoint formula between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is simply the average of the coordinates.\n\n7. We have two equations and we denote one point \\(A\\left( x_1, y_1 \\right)\\) and another \\(B\\left( x_2, y_2 \\right)\\). Since the origin is the midpoint:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_0 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_0 + y_2}{2} \\right) = (0,0)\n\\]\n\n8. This implies:\n\\[\n\\frac{x_0 + x_2}{2} = 0 \\implies x_0 + x_2 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x_2 = -x_0 = \\frac{24}{23}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{y_0 + y_2}{2} = 0 \\implies y_0 + y_2 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y_2 = -y_0 = \\frac{42}{23}\n\\]\n\n9. Hence, line \\(l\\) passes through the poi