cation with various sets, it was confirmed that the set \\(\\{-1, 0, 1, 2\\}\\) results in the smallest number of distinct values on the board, which is 6.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo find the smallest number of distinct integers that can appear on the board when considering all pairwise sums and products of four distinct integers, let's analyze the given integers and their derived sums and products.\n\n1. **Choosing the numbers \\(-1, 0, 1, 2\\)**:\n   - Pairwise sums:\n     \\[ \n     \\begin{align*}\n     -1 + 0 &= -1, \\\\\n     -1 + 1 &= 0, \\\\\n     -1 + 2 &= 1, \\\\\n      0 + 1 &= 1, \\\\\n      0 + 2 &= 2, \\\\\n      1 + 2 &= 3.\n     \\end{align*}\n     \\]\n     These sums yield: \\[-1, 0, 1, 2, 3\\].\n   - Pairwise products:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{align*}\n     -1 \\cdot 0 &= 0, \\\\\n     -1 \\cdot 1 &= -1, \\\\\n     -1 \\cdot 2 &= -2, \\\\\n      0 \\cdot 1 &= 0, \\\\\n      0 \\cdot 2 &= 0, \\\\\n      1 \\cdot 2 &= 2.\n     \\end{align*}\n     \\]\n     These products yield: \\(-2, -1, 0, 2\\).\n\n   Combining the unique sums and products, we get the set \\(\\{-2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3\\}\\), consisting of 6 different numbers.\n\n2. **Proving it cannot be fewer than 6 distinct numbers**:\n   Let the four distinct integers be \\(a < b < c < d\\).\n\n   - Pairwise sums are:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{align*}\n     a+b, \\, a+c, \\, a+d, \\, b+c, \\, b+d, \\, c+d\n     \\end{align*}\n     \\]\n     Since \\(a, b, c, d\\) are distinct and increasing, these sums are also distinct. So, at least 5 different sums must exist since there are 6 sums.\n\n   - To show that there must be a number different from these sums:\n     - Consider the case when \\(a \\geq 0\\).\n       \\[\n       \\begin{align*}\n       b &\\geq 1, \\\\\n       c &\\geq 2, \\\\\n       d &\\geq 3, \\text{ therefore} \\\\\n       cd &\\geq 2d > c + d.\n       \\end{align*}\n       \\]\n       This implies a number greater than \\(c + d\\) must exist.\n\n     - For \