 F(0) \\geq 0 \\).\n4. Define the set \\( D = \\{ x \\in M \\mid x \\leq F(x) \\} \\). Since \\( F(0) \\geq 0 \\), \\( 0 \\in D \\).\n5. Since \\( M \\) is a finite set containing \\( 0 \\), \\( D \\) is a finite subset of \\( M \\).\n6. Let \\( p = \\max D \\); thus, \\( p \\leq F(p) \\). This follows from the definition of \\( D \\).\n7. Using the fact that \\( F \\) is non-decreasing, \\( F(p) \\leq F(F(p)) \\).\n8. Consequently, we deduce that \\( F(p) \\in D \\).\n9. Given that \\( p \\) is the maximum of \\( D \\), it follows that \\( F(p) \\leq p \\).\n10. Combining the inequalities \\( p \\leq F(p) \\) and \\( F(p) \\leq p \\), we get \\( F(p) = p \\). Thus, \\( g(f(p)) = p \\).\n11. Therefore, there exists an integer \\( p \\) in \\( M \\) such that \\( g(f(p)) = p \\).\n\nConclusion. \\( \\boxed{g(f(p)) = p} \\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given the vectors \\(\\boldsymbol{a}=\\left(2 \\cos \\frac{x}{2}, \\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)\\) and \\(\\boldsymbol{b}=\\left(\\sqrt{2} \\sin \\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right), \\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)\\), let \\(f(x)=\\boldsymbol{a} \\cdot \\boldsymbol{b}\\). Find the maximum value of the function \\(f(x)\\), the smallest positive period, and determine the intervals of monotonicity of \\(f(x)\\) on \\(\\left[0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the maximum value, the smallest positive period, and the intervals of monotonicity of the function f(x) which is the dot product of vectors a and b. Let me start by writing down the vectors and the dot product formula.\n\nFirst, the vectors are given as:\na = (2 cos(x/2), tan(x/2 + \u03c0/4))\nb = (\u221a2 sin(x/2 + \u03c0/4), tan(x/2 - \u03c0/4))\n\nThe dot product f(x) = a \u00b7 b will be the sum of the products of their corresponding components. So that's:\n\nf(x) = [2 cos(x/2)] * [\u221a2