0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]\\).\n\nFirst, we compute the dot product \\(f(x)\\):\n\n\\[\nf(x) = \\left[2 \\cos \\frac{x}{2}\\right] \\left[\\sqrt{2} \\sin \\left(\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right] + \\left[\\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right] \\left[\\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right]\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the first term using trigonometric identities:\n\n\\[\n2 \\cos \\frac{x}{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\sin \\left(\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 2\\sqrt{2} \\cos \\frac{x}{2} \\sin \\left(\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n\\]\n\nUsing the identity \\(\\sin A \\cos B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\sin(A + B) + \\sin(A - B)]\\):\n\n\\[\n2\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\left[\\sin(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}) + \\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{4})\\right] = \\sqrt{2} \\left[\\sin(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}) + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right] = \\sqrt{2} \\sin(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}) + 1\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the second term using trigonometric identities and product-to-sum formulas:\n\n\\[\n\\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = -1\n\\]\n\nCombining both terms:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = \\sqrt{2} \\sin(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}) + 1 - 1 = \\sqrt{2} \\sin(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4})\n\\]\n\nThe maximum value of \\(\\sqrt{2} \\sin(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4})\\) is \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) since the maximum value of \\(\\sin\\) is 1. The period of \\(\\sin(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4})\\) is \\(2\\pi\\), so the smallest positive period of \\(f(x)\\) is \\(2\\pi\\).\n\nTo determine the intervals of monotonicity, we compute the derivative:\n\n\\[\nf'(x) = \\sqrt{2} \\cos(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4})\n\\]\n\nSetting \\(f'(x) = 0\\) gives the critical point \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\). On the interval \\([0, \\frac{\\pi}{4})\\), \\(\\cos(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4})\\) is positive, so \\(f(x)\\) is increasing. On the interval \\((\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}]\\), \\(\\cos(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4})\\) is negative, so \\(f(x)\\) is decreasing.\n\n### Final Answer\nThe maximum value of \\( f(x) \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{2}}\\), the smallest positive period is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi}\\), and the function is increasing on \\(\\left[0, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right]\\) and decreasing on \\(\\left[\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo solve this problem, we need to calculate the dot product of vectors $\\boldsymbol{a}$ and $\\boldsymbol{b}$, optimize the resulting function $f(x)$, determine its fundamental period, and identify the monotonic intervals within the specified domain.\n\n1. **Define the Vectors:**\n   Given the vectors:\n   \\[\n   \\boldsymbol{a}=\\left(2 \\cos \\frac{x}{2}, \\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\boldsymbol{b}=\\left(\\sqrt{2} \\sin \\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right), \\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Dot Product Calculation:**\n   The function $f(x)$ is defined as the dot product of $\\boldsymbol{a}$ and $\\boldsymbol{b}$:\n   \\[\n   f(x)=\\boldsymbol{a} \\cdot \\boldsymbol{b}=2 \\cos \\frac{x}{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\sin \\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + \\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\cdot \\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify Trigonometric Product:**\n   First, we simplify the term $2 \\sqrt{2} \\cos \\frac{x}{2} \\sin \\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$:\n   \\[\n   2 \\sqrt{2} \\cos \\frac{x}{2} \\sin \\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=2 \\cos \\frac{x}{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} (\\sin \\frac{x}{2} + \\cos \\frac{x}{2})\\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 2 \\cos \\frac{x}{2} (\\sin \\frac{x}{2} + \\cos \\frac{x}{2})\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplify the Tangent Product:**\n   Using the compound angle formula for tangent:\n   \\[\n   \\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\tan \\frac{x}{2} + 1}{1 - \\tan \\frac{x}{2}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\tan \\frac{x}{2} - 1}{1 + \\tan \\frac{x}{2}}\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   \\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\cdot \\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)= \\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\tan \\frac{x}{2} + 1}{1 - \\tan \\frac{x}{2}}\\right) \\left(\\frac{\\tan \\frac{x}{2} - 1}{1 + \\tan \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)}{1}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\left(\\frac{(\\tan \\frac{x}{2} + 1)(\\tan \\frac{x}{2} - 1)}{(1 - \\tan \\frac{x}{2})(1 + \\tan \\frac{x}{2})}\\right)\n   = -1\n   \\]\n\n5. **Combine and Simplify the Expression for \\( f(x) \\):**\n   \\[\n   f(x)=2 \\sqrt{2} \\cos \\frac{x}{2} \\sin \\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + \\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\cdot \\tan \\left(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 2 \\cos \\frac{x}{2} (\\sin \\frac{x}{2} + \\cos \\frac{x}{2}) + (-1)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 2 \\sin \\frac{x}{2} \\cos \\frac{x}{2} + 2 \\cos^2 \\frac{x}{2} - 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\sin x + \\cos x\n   \\]\n\n6. **Express \\( f(x) \\) in a Unified Form:**\n   Use the sum-to-product identities:\n   \\[\n   \\sin x + \\cos x = \\sqrt{2} \\sin \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n   Hence,\n   \\[\n   f(x) = \\sqrt{2} \\sin \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n7. **Determine Maximum and Minimum Values:**\n   The maximum value of $\\sin$ function is $1$, and minimum value is $-1$. Thus:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Maximum value of } f(x) = \\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Minimum value of } f(x) = -\\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Determine the Period:**\n   The period of $\\sin \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ is $2\\pi$:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Fundamental period of } f(x) = 2\\pi\n   \\]\n\n9. **Determine the Monotonic Intervals in \\([0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}]\\):**\n   The function $f(x) = \\sqrt{2} \\sin \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ increases in the interval:\n   \\[\n   \\left[0, \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right]\n   \\]\n   and decreases in the interval:\