s x-intercepts at 10 and 13, its vertex is at (11.5, c\u2081). So in vertex form, it's y = a\u2081(x - 11.5)^2 + c\u2081. Similarly, \u03a0\u2082 has vertex at (23, c\u2082), so y = a\u2082(x - 23)^2 + c\u2082.\n\nBut we know \u03a0\u2081 passes through (10,0) and (13,0). Let's plug in x=10 into \u03a0\u2081's vertex form:\n\n0 = a\u2081(10 - 11.5)^2 + c\u2081\n0 = a\u2081(2.25) + c\u2081\nSimilarly, x=13 gives the same equation because of symmetry. So c\u2081 = -2.25a\u2081.\n\nSimilarly, \u03a0\u2082 passes through (13,0). Plugging x=13 into \u03a0\u2082's vertex form:\n\n0 = a\u2082(13 - 23)^2 + c\u2082\n0 = a\u2082(100) + c\u2082\nSo c\u2082 = -100a\u2082\n\nBut from the midpoint condition, we have c\u2082 = 2c\u2081. So substituting:\n\n-100a\u2082 = 2*(-2.25a\u2081)\n-100a\u2082 = -4.5a\u2081\n100a\u2082 = 4.5a\u2081\na\u2082 = (4.5/100)a\u2081 = (9/200)a\u2081\n\nBut does this help us? Maybe not directly. However, the other x-intercept of \u03a0\u2082 is d. Let's recall that for a parabola in vertex form y = a(x - h)^2 + k, the roots can be found by solving a(x - h)^2 + k = 0 => (x - h)^2 = -k/a. Since the parabola opens upwards, a is positive, so real roots exist only if k is negative (which it is, as c\u2082 = -100a\u2082, and a\u2082 is positive, so c\u2082 is negative). The roots are h \u00b1 sqrt(-k/a). For \u03a0\u2082, the roots are 23 \u00b1 sqrt(-c\u2082/a\u2082). But we know one root is 13, so:\n\n23 - sqrt(-c\u2082/a\u2082) = 13\nsqrt(-c\u2082/a\u2082) = 10\nSquare both sides:\n(-c\u2082)/a\u2082 = 100\nBut from earlier, c\u2082 = -100a\u2082, so (-(-100a\u2082))/a\u2082 = 100a\u2082/a\u2082 = 100, which matches. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, since \u03a0\u2082's other root is d, and the distance from the vertex x=23 to each root is the same. Since one root is at 13, which is 10 units left of 23, the other root should be 10 units right of 23, which is 33. So that's another way to see it.\n\nTherefore, the other x-intercept is at x=33.\n\n