ac{\\sin 1}{2}\n\\]\n\n### Final Answers\n\n(1) \\(\\boxed{\\sin 1 \\leq f(x) + f(x+1) \\leq 2 \\cos \\frac{1}{2}}\\)\n\n(2) \\(\\boxed{\\sum_{k=n}^{3n-1} \\frac{f(k)}{k} > \\frac{\\sin 1}{2}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's address the problem parts step-by-step, providing detailed explanations and calculations.\n\n#### (1) Proof that $\\sin 1 \\leqslant f(x) + f(x+1) \\leqslant 2 \\cos \\frac{1}{2}$\n\n1. **Define the Function:**\n   \\[\n   g(x) = f(x) + f(x+1) = |\\sin x| + |\\sin (x+1)|\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Periodicity of \\( g(x) \\):**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Notice that } g(x) \\text{ is a periodic function with period } \\pi.\n   \\]\n   \n3. **Range Consideration:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Thus, we only need to consider } x \\in [0, \\pi].\n   \\]\n\n4. **Case 1: When \\( x \\in [0, \\pi-1] \\)**\n\n   \\[\n   x + \\frac{1}{2} \\in \\left[\\frac{1}{2}, \\pi - \\frac{1}{2}\\right]\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Rightarrow \\sin\\left(x + \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\in \\left[\\sin \\frac{1}{2}, 1\\right]\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   g(x) = \\sin x + \\sin(x + 1)\n   \\]\n   Using the sine addition formula:\n   \\[\n   \\sin(x + 1) = \\sin(x + \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}) = \\sin(x + \\frac{1}{2}) \\cos \\frac{1}{2} + \\cos(x + \\frac{1}{2}) \\sin \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n   Since:\n   \\[\n   g(x) = 2 \\sin\\left(x + \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\cdot \\cos \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n   And because:\n   \\[\n   \\sin\\left(x + \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\in \\left[\\sin \\frac{1}{2}, 1\\right]\n   \\]\n   It follows:\n   \\[\n   g(x) = 2 \\cos \\frac{1}{2} \\sin\\left(x + \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\in \\left[2 \\cos \\frac{1}{2} \\sin \\frac{1}{2}, 2 \\cos \\frac{1}{2}\\right] = [\\sin 1, 2 \\cos \\frac{1}{2}]\n   \\]\n\n5. **Case 2: When \\( x \\in [\\pi - 1, \\pi] \\)**\n\n   \\[\n   x + \\frac{1}{2} \\in \\left[\\pi - \\frac{1}{2}, \\pi + \\frac{1}{2}\\right]\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Rightarrow \\cos\\left(x + \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\in \\left[-1, -\\cos \\frac{1}{2}\\right]\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   g(x) = \\sin x